#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128936 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129125 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99c4151a-d2e0-5603-b27d-d81c76a0c7ba" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of Reciprocal Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "0203f79c-5170-51ac-8255-748dfd579c24" :question/math-academy-id "e-5297" :question/problem "For the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The graph passes through the point $\\left(4,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)$.\n2. The range of the function is $y \\in (-\\infty,0)\\cup (0,\\infty)$.\n3. $y \\rightarrow -\\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow -\\infty$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\n![](images/bb/bb7e6c981e7383bda679415437c5a250a82ff77e0708ac0f252f013ab38c25f0.png)\n\nNow, we analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. When $x=4$, we get $y=\\dfrac{1}{4}$, so the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}$ passes through the point $\\left(4,\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)$.\n- Statement II is true. The curve attains every real number except $0$.\n- Statement III is false. From the graph, we see that $y\\rightarrow 0$ as $x\\rightarrow -\\infty$.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128937 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129126 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27c4ce4c-7b03-5d9e-a1f0-be891c40f6a0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Graph of a Reciprocal Function"}] :question/id #uuid "3097e092-5889-5eab-9561-e331f2f71023" :question/math-academy-id "e-5298" :question/problem "Which of the following plots is the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}$?\n\n![](images/2e/2e6d2bd2ccad040cbc29116cf30a9201cbe6649de7457f28221e2aca1393a857.png)" :question/worked-solution "The graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac1x\n$$\n\nis shown below.\n\n![](images/ff/ffd98b8f9b6c7a9260f43772f9c654901bd02c21efa1eb06f2a5fdad8b263729.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}]}
